{-
   # extensions: avx2

   The 256-bit tier - Implementation-Vector.md §5.4 and §12's "the ymm/zmm tier".

   The directive above is the whole of what makes this fixture different from
   `VecValue.yana`, and it changes what the *types* mean rather than which instructions are picked:
   `targetVectorBytes` answers 32 under AVX2, so `Vec(Int)` here is eight lanes where the same
   source without the directive is four. That is why this is a file of its own with goldens of its
   own rather than a mode on an existing fixture.

   **Every assertion below is written to hold at any lane count**, which is what lets this run on
   JavaScript too - where a vector is sixteen bytes whatever the directive says, there being no
   register to read a width off. So a green run of this fixture is the statement §11.2 of the plan
   says the suite could not make: that the same program answers the same number at two different
   vector widths, on two backends, with a third (LLVM) holding its module.

   What it reaches on x64 that nothing else does: a value living in a ymm register and moving through
   one - a parameter, a result, a spill slot and a loop-carried phi - the packed arithmetic at
   VEX.256, a broadcast that has to cross the 128-bit halves (`vpbroadcastd`, since `vpshufd` works
   inside each half and cannot), and a reduction whose top level is the one cross-half shuffle this
   tier has. The lane access above the fourth is `VecWide.Lanes.yana`, for the reason recorded there.
-}

-- Read rather than written, but mutable, which is what stops the folder from seeing through it: a
-- mutable global's value is not a constant at any point in the program.
let &seed = 3 :: Int

fn doubled(v: Vec(Int)) -> Vec(Int) = v + v

fn scaled(v: Vec(Int), by: Int) -> Vec(Int) = v * splat(by)

{-
   A vector through a call the caller cannot see into, twice over, which is what puts one in the
   convention's registers and - with enough of them live at once - into a frame slot. A Slot256 is
   the only way a 32-byte value reaches memory, and `vmovups` is the only way it comes back.
-}
fn combined(a: Vec(Int), b: Vec(Int), c: Vec(Int), d: Vec(Int)) -> Int =
    lane(a + b, 0) + lane(c + d, 0)

fn main() -> Int:
    let base = splat(seed) :: Vec(Int)

    let twice = doubled(base)
    let six = scaled(base, 2)

    if lane(twice, 0) != 6 then return -1
    if lane(six, 1) != 6 then return -2

    {-
       The lane count, which every assertion below is written against rather than against a number.

       A lane *index* is checked where it is written and not where it runs - `constantLane` in
       simd.cpp reports "lane 5 is past the end of a vector of 4 lanes" during resolution - so a
       fixture cannot guard a high-lane access behind `if lanes > 4` and be compiled for a target
       whose vectors are narrower. That is why the wide lane access is asserted in
       `VecWide.Lanes.yana`, which has no JavaScript half at all, and why nothing here names an index
       above the first two.
    -}
    let lanes = horizontalSum(splat(1) :: Vec(Int))

    -- One lane different from the others, so the reduction folds something that is not the same
    -- number in every lane and the tree's *shape* is what produces the answer.
    let mixed = withLane(base, 0, 10)
    if horizontalMax(mixed) != 10 then return -3
    if horizontalMin(mixed) != 3 then return -4

    -- A loop-carried vector, which is what makes one live across a back edge and gives the allocator
    -- a phi to place. Four additions of `base` leave five times the seed in every lane, at any width.
    let &total = base :: Vec(Int)
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < 4:
        total = total + base
        i = i + 1

    if lane(total, 0) != 15 then return -5
    if lane(total, 1) != 15 then return -6

    -- The reduction over the whole register, whose top level at eight lanes is the cross-half swap
    -- `vperm2f128` expresses and whose lower levels are in-lane shuffles. Compared against the lane
    -- count the target reports rather than against a number.
    if horizontalSum(total) != lanes * 15 then return -7

    -- Four vectors live at once across a call, which is what reaches a wide spill slot.
    if combined(base, twice, six, total) != 3 + 6 + 6 + 15 then return -8

    -- The comparison into a mask, the mask family over one, and the lane-wise select - all at the
    -- wide register, and all width-independent in what they answer.
    let above = mixed .> base
    if count(above) != 1 then return -9
    if !any(above) then return -10
    if all(above) then return -11

    let picked = select(above, splat(7), splat(2)) :: Vec(Int)
    if lane(picked, 0) != 7 then return -12
    if lane(picked, 1) != 2 then return -13

    {-
       `firstSet` at the width where the sentinel does not fit beside the mask.

       On x64 this is a movemask and a bit scan, and the bit the scan finds for "nothing is set" is
       the one past the last lane *byte* - which at a whole 256-bit register is bit 32, and is not a
       bit an `i32` has. So the count is taken at 64 there and in 32 bits everywhere else, and these
       three assertions are what say the two agree: the lowest set lane, the answer where a mask is
       empty, and the answer where every lane is set.

       Lane 1 rather than lane 0 for the first of them, because a lane index is a *byte* index
       shifted here: the bit the scan finds for lane 1 of a four-byte lane is bit 4, and a shift
       dropped anywhere between the movemask and the answer reads as four rather than one.

       Written against `lanes` rather than against a number for the reason the rest of this fixture
       is: JavaScript has no register to read a width off, and the same source runs there at four.
    -}
    if firstSet(withLane(base, 1, 10) .> base) != 1 then return -14
    if firstSet(mixed .< base) != lanes then return -15
    if firstSet(mixed .>= base) != 0 then return -16

    return lane(twice, 1) * 100 + lane(six, 0) + lane(mixed, 0)
